condition_name: Apnea in a Child
condition_slug: apnea-in-a-child
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: Cessation of breath for more than 15 seconds or any period with bradycardia and/or cyanosis. There may also be hypotonia or pallor in severe cases. Particularly common among very preterm infants with estimated gestational age of < 32 weeks. It may also occur among sick term babies. Prematurity and immaturity of the central respiratory centre causes a separate entity known as “Apnea of Prematurity”.
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Common Features
    features[6]: Cessation of breath for more than 15 seconds,Bradycardia,Cyanosis,Hypotonia,Pallor,Particularly common among very preterm infants with estimated gestational age of < 32 weeks
investigations[1]: Investigate underlying causes
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[3]: Tactile stimulation such as flicking the sole of the feet,Apnea monitor or pulse oximeter with alarm system for hypoxaemia,Nursing in the prone position with cessation of oral feeds if gastro-oesophageal reflux is suspected
  drug[3]: 10% Dextrose-in-Water (4 mL/kg) for the correction of blood glucose,Caffeine citrate in a loading dose of 20 mg/kg oral or slow intravenous and maintenance of 5-10mg/kg per 24 hours,Aminophylline in a loading dose of 6mg/kg slow intravenous and maintenance of 2.5mg/kg 12-hourly until post-conceptional age of 33-34 weeks or until no longer requires oxygen therapy or has been free of apnea for at least 5 days
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[1]: "Resuscitation: airway clearance, assisted breathing and circulation according to standard protocols"
differential_diagnoses[11]: Septicaemia,Pneumonia,Necrotizing enterocolitis,Gastro-oesophageal reflux,Congestive cardiac failure,Meningitis,Intra-cranial haemorrhage,Seizures,Hypoglycaemia,Severe anaemia,Urinary tract infection
complications[0]:
prevention[1]: Early commencement of CPAP in a spontaneously breathing neonate
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null